<title>UZBEKISTAN: Uzbekistan clamps down on hard currency banks.</title>
<headline>Uzbekistan clamps down on hard currency banks.</headline>
<dateline>TASHKENT 1996-10-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Central Bank of Uzbekistan has reduced the number of banks licensed to handle foreign currency to two from 12, a central bank official said on Monday.</p>
<p>Only the Central Bank's foreign trading arm and Uzpromstroibank are now permitted to deal in foreign currency, the offical told Reuters.</p>
<p>The state-controlled press said the measures had been taken after &quot;serious offences&quot; in carrying out hard currency operations.</p>
<p>In an interview on Uzbek television on Saturday monitored by the British Broadcasting Company, Central Bank chairman Faizulla Mullajanov said the state sold an average of $500,000 each working day and said some bank officials had &quot;abused&quot; their positions.</p>
<p>--Tashkent newsroom, +3712 361958</p>
